<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Herbert Spencer</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1820">1820</a>–<a href="/years/1903">1903</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Herbert Spencer was an English polymath active as a philosopher, psychologist, biologist, sociologist and anthropologist. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derby">Derby</a>, he worked as a civil engineer and as sub-editor of The Economist, and published his first book, Social Statics, in 1851. He coined the expression survival of the fittest in Principles of Biology (1864), after reading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-darwin">Charles Darwin</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/on-the-origin-of-species">On the Origin of Species</a>, and spent most of his later life on his System of Synthetic Philosophy, which applied evolution to biology, psychology, sociology and morality. Of great authority in his lifetime, he lost influence sharply after 1900, and his name became closely linked with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/social-darwinism">Social Darwinism</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/herbert-spencer.jpg" alt="Herbert Spencer" width="330" height="509" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Herbert_Spencer.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Spencer was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derby">Derby</a> on 27 April 1820. His father, William George Spencer, a religious dissenter, ran a school based on the teaching methods of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-heinrich-pestalozzi">Johann Heinrich Pestalozzi</a> and served as secretary of the Derby Philosophical Society, founded in 1783 by Erasmus Darwin. Through its members Spencer met pre-Darwinian ideas of biological evolution, particularly those of Erasmus Darwin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-baptiste-lamarck">Jean-Baptiste Lamarck</a>. His uncle, the Reverend Thomas Spencer, taught him some mathematics, physics and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, and passed on firm free-trade and anti-statist views. Otherwise Spencer was an autodidact.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He worked as a civil engineer during the railway boom of the late 1830s while writing for radical provincial journals. While sub-editor of The Economist from 1848 to 1853 he published Social Statics, in which he predicted that humanity would become adapted to life in society and that the state would wither away. Through the publisher John Chapman's salon he knew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-stuart-mill">John Stuart Mill</a>, Harriet Martineau, George Henry Lewes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-eliot">Mary Ann Evans</a>, and he introduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-henry-huxley">Thomas Henry Huxley</a>, who became a lifelong friend. Lewes and Evans acquainted him with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auguste-comte">Auguste Comte</a>'s positivism, from which he strongly dissented.</p><p>Spencer set out his evolutionary view in the essay Progress: Its Law and Cause in 1857. He held that all structures in the universe develop from a simple, undifferentiated sameness to a complex, differentiated variety, a law he applied to stars, organisms, societies and the human mind. His agnosticism won him notoriety, though he said he wanted to reconcile science and religion.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In his last decades Spencer was lonely and disillusioned. He never married and after 1855 was a lifelong hypochondriac. He was nominated for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Literature in 1902, which went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodor-mommsen">Theodor Mommsen</a>, and he died on 8 December 1903 at the age of 83. By the 1870s and 1880s he may have been the only philosopher to sell over a million copies in his own lifetime, yet his influence declined sharply after 1900. His name is often linked with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/social-darwinism">Social Darwinism</a>, although such themes are absent from his sociological works, and libertarians have claimed him as a precursor.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Herbert Spencer” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The phrase survival of the fittest was coined by <b>Herbert Spencer</b>, not Darwin, and Spencer was not described as a social Darwinist until the 1930s, long after his death; Richard Hofstadter made the term a standard shorthand in the United States in 1944.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/social-darwinism">Social Darwinism · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Political commentators also drew on the book, among them <b>Herbert Spencer</b>, whom Darwin credited with the phrase survival of the fittest.</blockquote><a href="/work/on-the-origin-of-species">On the Origin of Species · Reception</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derby">Derby</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Brighton, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.92,-1.48&amp;year=1873" aria-label="Derby on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4517 341 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Herbert Spencer"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4967 611h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4967 611h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.92,-1.48&amp;year=1873">What was here in 1873? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;How to live?—that is the essential question for us. Not how to live in the mere material sense only, but in the widest sense.&#8221;</p><footer>What Knowledge Is of Most Worth? (1859), in Essays on Education &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/16510/pg16510-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;If every man has freedom to do all that he wills, provided he infringes not the equal freedom of any other man, then he is free to drop connection with the State,—to relinquish its protection and to refuse paying towards its support.&#8221;</p><footer>Social Statics (1851), ch. 19, The Right to Ignore the State &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/34649/pg34649-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
